### What is your issue?

Under the assumption that deterministic output is preferred whenever feasible, I'd like to point out that the variable names written into "coordinates" attributes with `.to_netcdf` are not ordered deterministically. For pipelines that depend on file hashes to validate dependencies, this can be a real headache.

Consider the dataset `xarray.Dataset({"x": ((), 0)}, coords={"a": 0, "b": 0})`. The NetCDF file XArray writes will include either:
```
variables:
	int64 x ;
		x:coordinates = "a b" ;
	int64 a ;
	int64 b ;
```
or
```
variables:
	int64 x ;
		x:coordinates = "b a" ;
	int64 a ;
	int64 b ;
```

My review of [`_encode_coordinates`](https://github.com/pydata/xarray/blob/52f5cf1fc521891008c4c968cee0b389211481c0/xarray/conventions.py#L631) leads me to think the behavior results from collecting names in a `set`. I'd be happy to offer a PR to make the coordinates attribute deterministic. I am not aware of a CF convention regarding any ordering, but would research and follow if it exists. If not, then I would probably `sort` at [L701](https://github.com/pydata/xarray/blob/52f5cf1fc521891008c4c968cee0b389211481c0/xarray/conventions.py#L701) and [L722](https://github.com/pydata/xarray/blob/52f5cf1fc521891008c4c968cee0b389211481c0/xarray/conventions.py#L722).

The NCL and geocat `heat_index` function are based off of [this NWS page](https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/html/heatindex_equation.shtml). There is ambiguous language on that page that has lead to a bug in NCL, which we replicated in geocat-comp.

From the NWS page, 

> The Rothfusz regression is not appropriate when conditions of temperature and humidity warrant a heat index value below about 80 degrees F. In those cases, a simpler formula is applied to calculate values consistent with Steadman's results:
HI = 0.5 * {T + 61.0 + [(T-68.0)*1.2] + (RH*0.094)}
In practice, the simple formula is computed first and the result averaged with the temperature. If this heat index value is 80 degrees F or higher, the full regression equation along with any adjustment as described above is applied.

This implies that the equation, `HI`, is averaged with temperature, which is what NCL did. However, that equation is already averaged with temperature.

